			<div class="content" id="post-content-4114097">
									

<p>Thank you very much! The interview logs are really tricky, especially subject 009 who I tried to write with a strange mix of informal speech and eloquence, to create a sense that he's a bit of an inept weirdo but that he revels in his own non-confrontational personality enough to be articulate about it. I'm worried that my attempts to use very casual speech don't ring authentic.<br />
For example I see you've corrected &quot;<em>and I went, like, whatever</em>&quot; to &quot;<em>and I was like whatever</em>&quot;. I know that stuff like &quot;<em>and I went 'no way' and then he went 'I swear to god'</em> &quot; exists, and that &quot;<em>like</em>&quot; is a common language tic, so I thought that maybe the combination &quot;<em>I went like [&#8230;]</em>&quot; was believable informal spoken English&#8230; I almost never speak English IRL, shit like this is really subtle if you've not spent months fully immersed among native speakers.</p>

